1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,960
This is STAT 331, video 10 b. We solve the exam problems on prediction. Video 10 a gave the theory.

2
00:00:09,410 --> 00:00:22,594
This video has five question types. Each type gets a method card and worked problems. Type A: a confidence interval for a mean response, or a prediction interval for a new response.

3
00:00:23,044 --> 00:00:26,116
Type B: decide if we can trust a prediction.

4
00:00:26,566 --> 00:00:31,302
Type C: compute the maximum likelihood estimates from summary numbers.

5
00:00:31,752 --> 00:00:35,656
Type D: pick the t quantile from given probabilities.

6
00:00:36,106 --> 00:00:41,375
Type E: a confidence interval or a test for beta one or beta zero.

7
00:00:41,825 --> 00:00:54,305
Section 1. Question type A gives a fitted model and a new x value. It asks for a confidence interval for the mean response, or a prediction interval for one new response.

8
00:00:54,755 --> 00:01:07,897
Method card A has five steps. Step 1: read beta zero hat, beta one hat, sigma hat and its degrees of freedom, n minus 2. Also read x bar, S x x and n.

9
00:01:08,347 --> 00:01:17,051
Step 2: the point estimate. Put x new into the fitted line. The green dot is mu zero hat, which is also y new hat.

10
00:01:17,501 --> 00:01:32,135
Step 3: the x part. It is 1 over n, plus x new minus x bar squared, over S x x. For a prediction interval, add 1, for the error of the new response. The bar shows the three pieces.

11
00:01:32,585 --> 00:01:42,953
Step 4: the standard error is sigma hat times the square root of the x part. The quantile is t 1 minus alpha over 2, n minus 2.

12
00:01:43,403 --> 00:01:56,033
Step 5: the interval is the estimate plus or minus t times the standard error. Check: the prediction interval, in orange, is wider than the confidence interval, in green.

13
00:01:56,483 --> 00:02:14,147
Tutorial 2, question 1. There are 25 patients. The response is satisfaction. The covariate is the severity of the illness. The question gives the R output of the fit. Parts d, e and f ask about severity 55 and 100.

14
00:02:14,597 --> 00:02:30,554
Step 1 of card A: read the output. The Estimate column gives the fitted line. Beta zero hat is 179.1312. Beta one hat is minus 2.3283.

15
00:02:31,004 --> 00:02:42,161
The residual standard error is sigma hat. It is 13.52, on 23 degrees of freedom. That is n minus 2, 25 minus 2.

16
00:02:42,611 --> 00:02:54,025
The question also prints the mean of severity, 50.48, and the sum of squared deviations, S x x, equal to 532.24.

17
00:02:54,475 --> 00:03:01,195
Now every number for parts d, e and f is known. Pause the video and try parts d and e.

18
00:03:01,645 --> 00:03:10,007
Part d. Pause the video and try it. We follow card A. Step 1 is done: the given values are in the bar at the top.

19
00:03:10,457 --> 00:03:24,047
Step 2: put x new equals 55 into the fitted line. Mu zero hat is 179.1312 minus 2.3283 times 55.

20
00:03:24,497 --> 00:03:39,259
2.3283 times 55 is 128.0565. So mu zero hat is 51.0747. The green dot on the line shows it.

21
00:03:39,709 --> 00:03:55,176
Step 3: the x part. Put in n equals 25, x bar equals 50.48, and S x x equals 532.24. The distance from x bar to 55 is 4.52.

22
00:03:55,626 --> 00:04:19,263
1 over 25 is 0.04. 4.52 squared is 20.4304, and 20.4304 over 532.24 is 0.03839. The x part is 0.07839. The bar shows the two pieces.

23
00:04:19,713 --> 00:04:32,641
Step 4: the standard error is sigma hat times the square root of the x part. 13.52 times 0.27998 is 3.7853.

24
00:04:33,091 --> 00:04:44,078
The quantile for 95 percent with 23 degrees of freedom is t 0.975, 23, which is 2.0687.

25
00:04:44,528 --> 00:05:00,464
Step 5: the interval is 51.0747 plus or minus 2.0687 times 3.7853. That is plus or minus 7.8307.

26
00:05:00,914 --> 00:05:21,757
So the 95 percent confidence interval for the mean satisfaction at severity 55 is 43.24 to 58.91. The posted solution uses unrounded estimates and gets 43.25 to 58.91. The difference comes from rounding.

27
00:05:22,207 --> 00:05:29,695
Part e: predict the satisfaction of one new patient with severity 55. Pause the video and try it.

28
00:05:30,145 --> 00:05:38,337
Step 2: the predicted value is the same number as in part d. Y new hat is 51.0747.

29
00:05:38,787 --> 00:05:57,837
Step 3: take the x part of part d, 0.07839, and add 1. The 1 comes from the error epsilon new of the new patient. The sum is 1.07839. In the bar, the 1 is much larger than the two other pieces.

30
00:05:58,287 --> 00:06:15,567
Step 4: the standard error for a prediction is 13.52 times the square root of 1.07839. That is 13.52 times 1.03846, which is 14.040.

31
00:06:16,017 --> 00:06:22,545
The level is 95 percent again, so the quantile is the same, 2.0687.

32
00:06:22,995 --> 00:06:37,801
Step 5: 51.0747 plus or minus 2.0687 times 14.040. That is plus or minus 29.045.

33
00:06:38,251 --> 00:07:00,117
So the 95 percent prediction interval is 22.03 to 80.12. The posted solution gives 22.04 to 80.12. The check of step 5: it is much wider than the confidence interval of part d, because one new patient also has the error epsilon new.

34
00:07:00,567 --> 00:07:08,162
Question type B asks if we can trust a prediction at a new x value. Method card B has four steps.

35
00:07:08,612 --> 00:07:15,375
Step 1: find the range of x in the data. That is the smallest and the largest observed x.

36
00:07:15,825 --> 00:07:23,547
Step 2: compare x new with this range. It also helps to measure the distance in standard deviations of x.

37
00:07:23,997 --> 00:07:30,376
Step 3: if x new is inside the range, the model was fitted and checked there. Use the interval.

38
00:07:30,826 --> 00:07:44,629
Step 4: if x new is outside the range, the prediction is an extrapolation. We have no data there. So linearity and constant variance were not checked there, and the interval is not trustworthy.

39
00:07:45,079 --> 00:07:52,631
Part f: should we trust a prediction at severity 100? Pause the video and try it. We follow card B.

40
00:07:53,081 --> 00:08:01,870
Step 1: the range of x. The posted solution says the largest observed severity is 62. The blue line marks it.

41
00:08:02,320 --> 00:08:08,784
Step 2: compare. 100 is greater than 62. The red region has no data.

42
00:08:09,234 --> 00:08:22,610
We can also measure the distance with S x x. The sample standard deviation of x is the square root of 532.24 over 24. That is 4.71.

43
00:08:23,060 --> 00:08:39,508
100 minus 50.48 is 49.52. 49.52 over 4.71 is 10.5. So severity 100 is 10.5 standard deviations above x bar.

44
00:08:39,958 --> 00:08:56,278
Step 4: x new is outside the range. The formula still gives a number. Y new hat is 179.1312 minus 2.3283 times 100, which is minus 53.70.

45
00:08:56,728 --> 00:09:16,973
The prediction interval also gives numbers. The x part plus 1 is 1, plus 0.04, plus 49.52 squared over 532.24, which is 4.6074. The sum is 5.6474.

46
00:09:17,423 --> 00:09:31,610
The standard error is 13.52 times the square root of 5.6474. That is 13.52 times 2.3764, which is 32.13.

47
00:09:32,060 --> 00:09:51,388
The interval is minus 53.70 plus or minus 2.0687 times 32.13. That is plus or minus 66.47, so minus 120.2 to 12.8. The red bar shows it.

48
00:09:51,838 --> 00:10:06,537
But we cannot trust it. We have no data near 100. So we cannot check that the relation is linear there, or that the variance is the same. The interval formula assumes that the model is correct at x new.

49
00:10:06,987 --> 00:10:20,853
Section 2. Question type C gives summary numbers, such as n, x bar and S x y, and asks for the maximum likelihood estimates. Lecture 4, practice question 1, is this type.

50
00:10:21,303 --> 00:10:39,223
Method card C has four steps. Step 1: for beta zero and beta one, the MLEs are the least squares estimates. Beta one hat is S x y over S x x. Beta zero hat is y bar minus beta one hat x bar.

51
00:10:39,673 --> 00:10:51,321
Step 2: for sigma squared, the MLE divides S S E by n. The unbiased estimate, sigma hat squared, divides S S E by n minus 2.

52
00:10:51,771 --> 00:11:07,430
Step 3: some quantities are missing. Write each one with a short form. S x x equals n minus 1 times s x squared. S x y equals the sum of x i y i, minus n x bar y bar.

53
00:11:07,880 --> 00:11:16,392
Step 4: solve for the missing value. Then compute in order: first y bar, then beta one hat, then beta zero hat.

54
00:11:16,842 --> 00:11:28,447
Lecture 4, practice question 1 a. Write the MLEs with x bar, y bar, S x y and the sample variance of x. Pause the video and try it.

55
00:11:28,897 --> 00:11:42,743
The sample variance is missing from the formulas, so we start with step 3 of card C. By definition, s x squared is the sum of x i minus x bar squared, over n minus 1.

56
00:11:43,193 --> 00:11:54,457
The sum of x i minus x bar squared is S x x. So s x squared is S x x over n minus 1. The blue bar is S x x.

57
00:11:54,907 --> 00:12:08,027
Multiply by n minus 1. S x x equals n minus 1 times s x squared. The bar is cut into n minus 1 equal pieces, and each piece is s x squared.

58
00:12:08,477 --> 00:12:23,453
Step 1: the MLE of beta one is the least squares estimate, S x y over S x x. Put in line 3. Beta one hat is S x y over n minus 1 times s x squared.

59
00:12:23,903 --> 00:12:31,007
The MLE of beta zero is y bar minus beta one hat x bar. These are the answers.

60
00:12:31,457 --> 00:12:39,947
Part b. For n equals 20, find the ratio of sigma hat to sigma hat MLE. Pause the video and try it.

61
00:12:40,397 --> 00:12:55,053
Step 2 of card C. The unbiased estimate divides S S E by n minus 2. Here that is 18. In the picture, S S E is cut into 18 pieces, and sigma hat squared is one piece.

62
00:12:55,503 --> 00:13:01,818
The MLE divides the same S S E by n, which is 20. So its piece is smaller.

63
00:13:02,268 --> 00:13:10,396
Divide line 1 by line 2. S S E cancels. The ratio of the variances is n over n minus 2.

64
00:13:10,846 --> 00:13:18,270
Take the square root. Sigma hat over sigma hat MLE is the square root of n over n minus 2.

65
00:13:18,720 --> 00:13:29,856
With n equals 20, this is the square root of 20 over 18, which is the square root of 1.1111. That is 1.0541.

66
00:13:30,306 --> 00:13:40,909
Part c. We have n, S x x, x bar, S x y, and the sum of x i y i. Y bar is not given. Pause the video and try it.

67
00:13:41,359 --> 00:13:53,199
Step 3 of card C: write S x y with a short form. Start from the definition: the sum of x i minus x bar, times y i minus y bar.

68
00:13:53,649 --> 00:14:02,417
Multiply out. There are four terms. The sums of x i and of y i are underlined, because the next line replaces them.

69
00:14:02,867 --> 00:14:12,979
The sum of x i is n x bar, and the sum of y i is n y bar. So the two middle terms are both minus n x bar y bar.

70
00:14:13,429 --> 00:14:19,679
Collect. S x y equals the sum of x i y i, minus n x bar y bar.

71
00:14:20,129 --> 00:14:28,897
Step 4: solve this for y bar. Y bar is the sum of x i y i minus S x y, over n x bar.

72
00:14:29,347 --> 00:14:48,505
Put in the numbers. 3378 minus 353 is 3025. 50 times 4.8 is 240. 3025 over 240 is 12.60417.

73
00:14:48,955 --> 00:15:00,005
Then beta one hat is S x y over S x x. 353 over 180 is 1.96111.

74
00:15:00,455 --> 00:15:25,991
Last, beta zero hat is y bar minus beta one hat x bar. 1.96111 times 4.8 is 9.41333. 12.60417 minus 9.41333 is 3.19084, about 3.1908. The chain on the right shows the order.

75
00:15:26,441 --> 00:15:38,260
Section 3. In an exam, you get no t table. The question gives some probabilities of a t distribution instead. Question type D: pick the right quantile from them.

76
00:15:38,710 --> 00:15:43,723
Method card D. Step 1: the degrees of freedom are n minus 2.

77
00:15:44,173 --> 00:15:59,576
Step 2: a 100 times 1 minus alpha percent interval needs t 1 minus alpha over 2, n minus 2. That is the point with area 1 minus alpha over 2 to its left. The green area shows it.

78
00:16:00,026 --> 00:16:16,218
Step 3: write each given probability as an area to the left of a point c. Use symmetry. The probability that T is above minus c equals the probability that T is below c. Each red tail has area alpha over 2.

79
00:16:16,668 --> 00:16:22,599
Step 4: match the area with 1 minus alpha over 2. That point is the quantile.

80
00:16:23,049 --> 00:16:38,707
Lecture 4, practice question 2. The question gives n, the estimates, S x x, x bar and sigma hat. It also gives three probabilities for t with 8 degrees of freedom. Pause the video and pick the quantiles.

81
00:16:39,157 --> 00:16:45,963
Step 1 of card D: the degrees of freedom are n minus 2, which is 10 minus 2, which is 8.

82
00:16:46,413 --> 00:17:01,154
Step 2: parts a and b ask for 95 percent intervals, so they need area 0.975 to the left. Part c asks for 90 percent, so it needs area 0.95 to the left.

83
00:17:01,604 --> 00:17:11,887
Step 3: the first probability. The probability that T is above minus 1.86 is 0.95. The blue area shows it.

84
00:17:12,337 --> 00:17:29,809
Mirror the picture. By symmetry, the probability that T is below 1.86 is also 0.95. This matches 0.95, so t 0.95, 8 is 1.86. Part c uses it.

85
00:17:30,259 --> 00:17:48,029
The third probability. The probability that T is below 2.31 is 0.975. This matches 0.975, so t 0.975, 8 is 2.31. Parts a and b use it.

86
00:17:48,479 --> 00:18:10,239
The second probability. The probability that the absolute value of T is above 2.75 is 0.025. Each tail has 0.0125, so the area to the left of 2.75 is 0.9875. No part of the question needs it.

87
00:18:10,689 --> 00:18:24,620
Question type E asks for a confidence interval for beta one or beta zero, or for a test of beta one equals 0. Video 8 gave the theory. Method card E has four steps.

88
00:18:25,070 --> 00:18:41,155
Step 1: the standard error. For beta one hat, it is sigma hat over the square root of S x x. For beta zero hat, it is sigma hat times the square root of 1 over n plus x bar squared over S x x.

89
00:18:41,605 --> 00:18:48,027
Step 2: the quantile t 1 minus alpha over 2, n minus 2. Card D finds it.

90
00:18:48,477 --> 00:18:57,010
Step 3: the confidence interval is beta j hat plus or minus t times its standard error. The green bar shows it.

91
00:18:57,460 --> 00:19:18,047
Step 4: the test of H 0, beta j equals 0. The t statistic is beta j hat over its standard error. The p-value is 2 times the probability that t n minus 2 is above the absolute value of t. Reject H 0 if the p-value is less than alpha.

92
00:19:18,497 --> 00:19:37,419
Parts a and b. Pause the video and try them. We follow card E. Step 1: the standard error of beta one hat is sigma hat over the square root of S x x. 0.4 over the square root of 16 is 0.4 over 4, which is 0.1.

93
00:19:37,869 --> 00:19:48,451
Step 2: for 95 percent, the quantile is t 0.975, 8, which is 2.31. We found it on the last slide.

94
00:19:48,901 --> 00:20:04,367
Step 3: the interval is 5 plus or minus 2.31 times 0.1. That is 5 plus or minus 0.231, so 4.769 to 5.231.

95
00:20:04,817 --> 00:20:22,545
Part b. Step 1: the standard error of beta zero hat is sigma hat times the square root of 1 over n, plus x bar squared over S x x. Put in n equals 10, x bar equals 0, and S x x equals 16.

96
00:20:22,995 --> 00:20:39,251
X bar is 0, so the second term is 0. 0.4 times the square root of 0.1 is 0.4 times 0.3162, which is 0.1265.

97
00:20:39,701 --> 00:20:56,555
Step 3: minus 2 plus or minus 2.31 times 0.1265. That is minus 2 plus or minus 0.292, so minus 2.292 to minus 1.708.

98
00:20:57,005 --> 00:21:01,741
The green box gives both answers. The number lines show both intervals.

99
00:21:02,191 --> 00:21:07,183
Part c is question type A again, with card A. Pause the video and try it.

100
00:21:07,633 --> 00:21:15,611
Step 2: the predicted value at x new equals 5 is minus 2 plus 5 times 5. That is 23.

101
00:21:16,061 --> 00:21:34,344
Step 3: a prediction, so the x part gets the 1. 1, plus 1 over 10, plus 5 minus 0 squared over 16. That is 1 plus 0.1 plus 1.5625, which is 2.6625.

102
00:21:34,794 --> 00:21:50,666
Step 4: the standard error is 0.4 times the square root of 2.6625. That is 0.4 times 1.6317, which is 0.6527.

103
00:21:51,116 --> 00:22:01,868
A 90 percent interval has alpha equal to 0.1, so it needs t 0.95, 8. From card D, it is 1.86.

104
00:22:02,318 --> 00:22:13,134
Step 5: 23 plus or minus 1.86 times 0.6527. That is plus or minus 1.214.

105
00:22:13,584 --> 00:22:21,413
So the 90 percent prediction interval is 21.786 to 24.214.

106
00:22:21,863 --> 00:22:36,306
Section 4: one full problem, Assignment 1, question 3. There are 90 berries. x is the sugar content in grams per litre. y is the chewiness in millijoules. The question has five parts.

107
00:22:36,756 --> 00:22:46,953
Part a is the scatterplot. The sugar values take only 6 levels, from 176.5 to 258.5.

108
00:22:47,403 --> 00:22:53,718
Part b asks for the least squares estimates and their meaning. Video 4 has the cards for this.

109
00:22:54,168 --> 00:22:58,733
Part c is a test of beta one equals 0. That is card E.

110
00:22:59,183 --> 00:23:05,455
Part d is a confidence interval for the mean chewiness at 220. That is card A.

111
00:23:05,905 --> 00:23:16,679
Part e is a prediction interval at 110, and a reason to mistrust it. That is card A, then card B. Pause the video and try the question.

112
00:23:17,129 --> 00:23:31,550
Part b. Card B of video 4 computes the estimates. Step 1: from the data file, x bar is 217.333 and y bar is 2.70833.

113
00:23:32,000 --> 00:23:42,645
Step 2: S x x is 70657.4, and S x y is minus 1610.78.

114
00:23:43,095 --> 00:23:59,863
Step 3: beta one hat is S x y over S x x. Minus 1610.78 over 70657.4 is minus 0.022797.

115
00:24:00,313 --> 00:24:28,793
Step 4: beta zero hat is y bar minus beta one hat x bar. 0.022797 times 217.333 is 4.95454. 2.70833 plus 4.95454 is 7.66287, about 7.6629. The green line is the fitted line.

116
00:24:29,243 --> 00:24:38,097
Now card C of video 4 interprets them. Step 1: the units. x is in grams per litre, and y is in millijoules.

117
00:24:38,547 --> 00:24:48,595
Step 2: for each additional gram per litre of sugar, the estimated mean chewiness decreases by 0.0228 millijoules.

118
00:24:49,045 --> 00:25:02,357
Step 3: beta zero hat, 7.6629 millijoules, is the estimated mean chewiness at sugar content 0. It is the height of the line at x equals 0.

119
00:25:02,807 --> 00:25:18,359
Step 4: the sugar values in the data go from 176.5 to 258.5. Sugar 0 is far outside this range. So beta zero hat has no practical meaning here.

120
00:25:18,809 --> 00:25:40,611
Part c. Pause the video and try it. Card E, step 1. Sigma hat is the square root of S S E over n minus 2. From the data, S S E is 74.126. Over 88, that is 0.84234, and its square root is 0.9178.

121
00:25:41,061 --> 00:26:00,901
The standard error of beta one hat is 0.9178 over the square root of 70657.4. That is 0.9178 over 265.81, which is 0.003453.

122
00:26:01,351 --> 00:26:22,983
Step 4: the t statistic is beta one hat minus 0, over its standard error. Minus 0.022797 over 0.003453 is minus 6.60. The blue line marks it on the t density with 88 degrees of freedom.

123
00:26:23,433 --> 00:26:39,284
The test is two-sided. The p-value is 2 times the probability that t 88 is above 6.60. That is 3.0 times 10 to the minus 9: the area beyond plus and minus 6.60.

124
00:26:39,734 --> 00:26:54,625
Step 2 gives the same decision. The critical value t 0.975, 88 is 1.987, and the absolute value of t, 6.60, is much larger.

125
00:26:55,075 --> 00:27:09,475
The p-value is less than 0.05, so we reject H 0 at the 5 percent level. The data give strong evidence of a linear association between sugar content and mean chewiness.

126
00:27:09,925 --> 00:27:21,125
Part d. Pause the video and try it. We follow card A. Step 1 is in the bar: the estimates, sigma hat, x bar, S x x and n come from the data.

127
00:27:21,575 --> 00:27:40,561
Step 2: mu zero hat is 7.66288 minus 0.022797 times 220. The product is 5.01534, so mu zero hat is 2.6475.

128
00:27:41,011 --> 00:27:56,393
Step 3: the x part. 1 over 90 is 0.011111. 220 minus 217.3333, squared, is 7.1111.

129
00:27:56,843 --> 00:28:14,229
7.1111 over 70657.4 is 0.000101. The sum is 0.011212. In the bar, the second piece is very small.

130
00:28:14,679 --> 00:28:33,517
Step 4: the standard error is 0.9178 times the square root of 0.011212. That is 0.9178 times 0.10589, which is 0.09719.

131
00:28:33,967 --> 00:28:41,796
The quantile for 95 percent with 88 degrees of freedom is 1.9873.

132
00:28:42,246 --> 00:28:56,923
Step 5: 2.6475 plus or minus 1.9873 times 0.09719. That is plus or minus 0.1931.

133
00:28:57,373 --> 00:29:09,064
So the 95 percent confidence interval is 2.454 to 2.841. It is narrow, because 220 is close to x bar.

134
00:29:09,514 --> 00:29:17,237
Part e: predict the chewiness of a berry with 110 grams per litre of sugar. Pause the video and try it.

135
00:29:17,687 --> 00:29:36,972
Card A, step 2: y new hat is 7.66288 minus 0.022797 times 110. The product is 2.50767, so y new hat is 5.1552.

136
00:29:37,422 --> 00:29:50,755
Step 3: a prediction, so the x part gets the 1. 110 minus 217.3333, squared, is 11520.44.

137
00:29:51,205 --> 00:30:08,784
11520.44 over 70657.4 is 0.163046. The sum is 1.174157. The bar shows the three pieces.

138
00:30:09,234 --> 00:30:28,647
Step 4: the standard error is 0.9178 times the square root of 1.174157. That is 0.9178 times 1.08359, which is 0.99452.

139
00:30:29,097 --> 00:30:47,380
Step 5: 5.1552 plus or minus 1.9873 times 0.99452. That is plus or minus 1.9764, so 3.179 to 7.132.

140
00:30:47,830 --> 00:30:57,067
Now card B: why might we mistrust this interval? Step 1: the smallest sugar value in the data is 176.5.

141
00:30:57,517 --> 00:31:07,523
Step 2: 110 is less than 176.5, so x new is outside the range. The red region has no data.

142
00:31:07,973 --> 00:31:21,605
Step 4: this is extrapolation. We cannot check the linear model, or the constant variance, below 176.5. So we mistrust the interval, as in Tutorial 2 part f.

143
00:31:22,055 --> 00:31:38,417
To recap. Card A gives an interval at a new x: read the output, compute y hat, the x part, plus 1 for a prediction, then the standard error and the quantile, and last the estimate plus or minus t times the standard error.

144
00:31:38,867 --> 00:31:46,825
Card B decides if we can trust it: inside the range of the data, use it. Outside, it is extrapolation.

145
00:31:47,275 --> 00:31:55,061
Card C computes the MLEs from summary numbers, with the short forms of S x x and S x y.

